n$ python -V\nPython 3.7.3\n$ pip list | grep setup\nsetuptools                    41.2.0\n```\n\nMy setuptools version is newer than yours, but I don't know what it was at the time I made that upload.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1573229577,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU1MTg4OTQwNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3808#issuecomment-551889405"},"message":"\u003e actually that rc5 was cut by @kyleam so it might be something specific about his setup.\n\nBased on trying this with a fresh debian install, I'd say the sdist produced by my setup is in line with the default.\n\n```\n$ docker run -it --rm debian:buster\n# apt-get update \u0026\u0026 apt-get install -y git python3 python3-setuptools\n# git clone https://github.com/datalad/datalad \u0026\u0026 cd datalad\n# python3 setup.py sdist\n#\n# tar --list -f dist/datalad-0.12.0rc6.tar.gz | grep setup\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/setup.cfg\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/setup.py\n#\n# tar --list -f dist/datalad-0.12.0rc6.tar.gz | grep build\n```\n\nSo it seems that there's something undocumented about your environment and/or the release procedure that produces the sdist files you're after.  If I had to guess, I'd say you have `setuptools_scm` installed:\n\n```\n# git clean -xfd\n# apt-get install python3-setuptools-scm\n# python3 setup.py sdist\n#\n# tar --list -f dist/datalad-0.12.0rc6.tar.gz | grep setup\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/setup.py\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/setup.cfg\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/setup.py\n#\n# tar --list -f dist/datalad-0.12.0rc6.tar.gz | grep build\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/.github/workflows/docbuild.yml\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/__init__.py\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/formatters.py\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/setup.py\n```\n\nThat output at least matches what I see with the 0.12.0rc6 tarball on pypi:\n\n```\n$ wget https://files.pythonhosted.org/packages/7f/b2/6699d40b34fdf47d9ec2f42d8bc67b3cce68f36d4af305fa6f849adddf40/datalad-0.12.0rc6.tar.gz\n$\n$ tar --list -f datalad-0.12.0rc6.tar.gz | grep setup\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/setup.py\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/setup.py\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/setup.cfg\n$\n$ tar --list -f datalad-0.12.0rc6.tar.gz | grep build\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/.github/workflows/docbuild.yml\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/__init__.py\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/formatters.py\ndatalad-0.12.0rc6/_datalad_build_support/setup.py\n```\n\n---\n\nAssuming the above is true, I'd say the two main options are either to\n\n  * document that `setuptools_scm` is required and add it to `setup_requires`\n  * avoid using `setuptools_scm` and add a MANIFEST.in that has at least `include _datalad_build_support/*.py`","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1573344098,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU1MjE0ODcwOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3808#issuecomment-552148709"},"message":"\u003e I'd say you have `setuptools_scm` installed:\n\nmost likely it was the case! (for 5 year old Debian laptop, my good old friend hopa indeed had lots of things installed).  I will check when/if I boot it up again some time ;)  **THANK YOU for digging!**\n\nSince we don't yet use a proper new fashioned setuptools+friends setup with pyproject.toml, I guess the best would be to rely on MANIFEST.in","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578736150,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDU3MzMwMTE5Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3808#issuecomment-573301196"},"message":"Given this issue refers to an outdated RC, I will close it now.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1578736150,"metadata":{"gi